tt also  i suppose the kremlin is probably  concerned about the geography of these  protests uh 18 or 19 cities in russia  count population of uh one million or  more but the geography is much wider  it's i think about 50 cities where the  arrests were made that means that there  are  russian-style fairly small places like  places with  300 400 000 population  in such places people who protest are  much better now  and the protest spreads much quicker  protests or at least news of protests  spread much quicker if you combine it  with the  impending i think redundancies huge  redundancies at uh  factories that close at businesses that  close  eventually what may shake the foundation  it's not the peace movement as such  but the peace movement combined with  workers that are discontent that may be  a much bigger problem for putin than the  big city intelligence students that are  mostly those who rally against the war  now okay so it's a dangerous situation  for citizens who might be against the  war i also want to ask you about those  small places you mentioned in russia i  mean are they feeling the effects of  russia's invasion of ukraine  not yet but  the speed with which  basically western business withdraws  from russia  uh will also mean that a lot of russian  companies will collapse that is  inevitable  just to give you an example and it's  something that i've only seen probably  once in my lifetime in 1998 when russia  defaulted on its debt international debt  uh the uh uh the currency uh currency  rates today are actually well the dollar  and the ruble gained a hundred percent  over what they used to be let's say 10  days ago  so it's  really something that is going to impact  russia which was built into the global  economy very much so okay you have the  adidas or  let us say what uh major car makers  withdrawing but they were served by a  lot of other companies which are russian  companies so  these companies are inevitably going to  go out of business or slash style that  means that lots of people will feel  what's happening quite soon but probably  not really tomorrow and the state the  russian state will have a big problem  dealing with that too because there's a  lot of people discontent momentarily  okay they will be feeling the pinch then  dw's russian affairs analyst constantine  edgar thanks so much for joining us on  the show  how are russians viewing this invasion  let's put that to our russia analyst  roman gonzalenko he joins me now uh is  there a possibility that russians could  stop vladimir putin's war in ukraine  well a lot of ukrainians hoped for that  but i would be very very careful  well we've seen that uh russian  propaganda is very effective it is  working and it has been working for  years so um when we look at the state  polls in russia they show that up to  three-fourths of the russian population  actually support vladimir's putin  actions in ukraine  some  independent polls  make a different picture but not very  much so we've seen for example um  estimates of the  uh levada center in moscow um they say  um up to two-thirds of russian so 60  plus  support the current uh russian actions  in ukraine support this war so i would  say um it is possible that russians  could stop vladimir putin but but there  are a few russians who can do that it's  a minority  and domestically what effect has this  invasion had on russian  society well this effect is very much um  underestimated i would say especially in  um certain areas like for example spare  parts for cars or western car  manufacturers or in general i would say  this this tendency when western  companies are leaving russia i think  this is something that no one really  expected when they planned this  operation i think this is something that  is hitting russians very much and um  also that the west is  excluding russian from from air travel  blocking russian ships um worldwide  sending russian  athletes home not allowing them to  participate in in in competitions this  is something i think that is that the  russia underestimated and this influence  will grow it will it will grow and the  frustration within the russian society  will also grow  i want to ask you more about the west  response response because the u.s and  poland they've been discussing the  delivery of fighter jets to ukraine does  that signal nato's direct involvement in  this conflict  well it's disputable russia has been  warning nato not to do such things and  we've seen today  new warnings from the russian foreign  minister the spokeswoman said that  sending troops to ukraine western  western fighters who would fight on the  ukrainian side or  bringing um fighter jets to ukraine  sending this this kind of weapons to  ukraine would lead to a catastrophe she  said so there is a clear warning on the  russian side from president putin and  from the foreign minister not to do that  but on the other hand i've spoken to a  an expert today a british expert and he  said we have to try this we have to try  this and see what russian reaction would  be because we've seen a deliveries to  ukraine of western weapons and um it it  hasn't been um  there was no no strong reaction from  from russia i would say so russia  expected possibly things like that but  the reaction has been relatively  moderate  dw's russia analyst roman goncharenko  thank you so much for joining us with  that update  you
 

